<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charminar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1591">1591</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Charminar is a monument and mosque in the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, built in 1591 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-quli-qutb-shah">Muhammad Quli Qutb Shah</a> of the Golconda Sultanate when he founded the city. Its square body is crowned at each corner by a minaret about 56 metres high, which gives the building its name, 'four minarets'. It is the best-known symbol of Hyderabad.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charminar.jpg" alt="Charminar" width="320" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DidierTais · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charminar_Hyderabad_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
